# Every variable this service reads, and nothing else.
#
# Rule 9 of docs/ecosystem/03 §2: a repository declares the variables it needs and the deploy
# provides exactly those. `env_file: .env` fan-out — which hands every container the whole estate's
# secrets — is banned, and this file is what makes the alternative possible.
#
# Copy to `.env` for local work. `.env` is gitignored and must stay that way.
#
# Every value below is a PLACEHOLDER. Nothing here is a credential, and `src/env.ts` refuses to
# start on a known placeholder rather than booting with one — a placeholder that boots is a
# placeholder that reaches production.

# --- required --------------------------------------------------------------------------------

# This service's own database. It reads no other, and CI fails on a second connection string.
LANTERN_DATABASE_URL=postgres://lantern:lantern@127.0.0.1:5432/lantern

# ── SERVING BOTH NETWORKS FROM ONE POD ────────────────────────────────────────
#
# Both OPTIONAL and empty here: a development stack is one network. See
# micro-deploy `docs/network-consolidation.md`.
#
# Set the second DSN and this deployment holds a handle per network, choosing per
# request from the `CF-Network` header the gateway stamps. Empty, `networkSql`
# holds one handle and REFUSES a testnet request rather than answering it out of
# mainnet rows.
LANTERN_DATABASE_URL_TESTNET=
# ── WHICH ESTATE THIS DEPLOYMENT IS ───────────────────────────────────────────
#
# REQUIRED in the estate, and the render sets it for every pod. It was once
# documented here as "never in production" and that was wrong twice over:
#
#   1. The pod keys its own database handle by it. Without it a testnet
#      deployment registers its testnet DSN under the name `mainnet` and then
#      refuses every request stamped `CF-Network: testnet`.
#
#   2. SERVICE-TO-SERVICE CALLS NEVER REACH THE GATEWAY. An outbox relay POSTing
#      to another service goes container to container; nothing stamps a header
#      on it and nothing can. Without a declared network those answer 500.
#
# The header still WINS when present, so this cannot mask a mis-stamped request
# from outside — it only answers the internal callers that never had one. Leave
# it empty only where a pod genuinely serves both estates.
CF_NETWORK_SINGLE=


# Identity's JWKS and issuer, for the operator read routes. Token verification is the only thing
# this service asks identity for — and identity being unreachable does NOT lock you out: see
# LANTERN_TOKEN below.
IDENTITY_JWKS_URL=http://127.0.0.1:4001/.well-known/jwks.json
IDENTITY_ISSUER=http://127.0.0.1:4001

# ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
# THE CREDENTIAL WHOSE ABSENCE IS A SUPPORTED MODE IN THE SERVICE THIS REPLACES.
#
# The frozen service defaults NIMBUS_JWKS_URL and LANTERN_TOKEN to the empty string
# (stack/infra/lantern/src/env.js:35,43), and that pair of empty strings makes authMode() return
# 'open' (src/auth.js:24-31,42) — which serves every log line in the estate to anyone who can reach
# the port, after a single boot-time warning nobody re-reads. Here LANTERN_TOKEN is REQUIRED and its
# SHAPE is asserted by @cloudsforge/secrets at boot.
#
# It is presented in an `x-lantern-token` header — the same name the frozen service already reads
# (src/auth.js:49) — and it gates /metrics, which publishes which services are failing and how fast.
# It is checked BEFORE the identity token so that identity being down does not make the service
# unreadable, which is exactly when someone is reading it.
#
# Generate one: openssl rand -base64 32
#
# NOT "at least 24 characters" — that floor is gone, and its removal is micro-org #142. It could
# never fail for `estate-only-lantern-token-000000000000`, which is 38 characters and is the value
# the compose file hardcodes on two lines today. @cloudsforge/secrets refuses a placeholder MARKER
# anywhere in the value (punctuation and case stripped first, so `estate-only`, `ESTATE_ONLY` and
# `estateonly` are one rule), a JWT, anything under 16 characters, and anything degenerate enough to
# fail a Shannon entropy floor. It does NOT dictate an alphabet: this token is transcribed out of a
# runbook by whoever is holding the incident, so a value you chose yourself is accepted — a
# placeholder you chose is not. Leaving CHANGE_ME here is refused at boot, deliberately.
# ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
LANTERN_TOKEN=CHANGE_ME

# --- optional, with the defaults shown ---------------------------------------------------------

PORT=4010
NODE_ENV=development
LOG_LEVEL=info
LANTERN_DATABASE_POOL_MAX=10

# Names this replica in jobs.locked_by. Defaults to the hostname, which is the container id under
# compose and the pod name under Kubernetes.
# INSTANCE_ID=

# The image tag, reported on every log line.
# CLOUDSFORGE_TAG=dev

# --- the ingest limits — the hostile-input boundary --------------------------------------------
#
# Every limit REFUSES rather than repairs: a truncated protobuf is a different protobuf, so a body
# over the size ceiling is a 413 with nothing read, not a shortened message.
# LANTERN_MAX_BODY_BYTES=4194304
# LANTERN_MAX_RECORDS=5000
# LANTERN_MAX_ATTRIBUTES=128
# LANTERN_MAX_DEPTH=8
# LANTERN_MAX_STRING_BYTES=8192

# --- retention ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# An issue outlives its events — 90 over 7 — and boot refuses the inversion. Loki holds the raw
# stream; seven days here is a triage horizon, not an archive. Rollups are small and outlive both,
# which is what a 400-day error-rate trend is computed from (Prometheus cannot downsample).
# LANTERN_EVENT_RETENTION_DAYS=7
# LANTERN_ISSUE_RETENTION_DAYS=90
# LANTERN_ROLLUP_RETENTION_DAYS=400
# LANTERN_RUM_RETENTION_DAYS=30

# --- the browser RUM sink ----------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# `name=url` origins allowed to POST client telemetry. EMPTY means the sink is OFF — "a browser may
# post to us" is a decision somebody makes, not a thing that happens because nobody set a variable.
# Never `*`: this is the one route that answers without a credential, and a wildcard there lets any
# page on the internet write into the triage view. The sink holds NO user_id, by policy.
# LANTERN_RUM_ORIGINS=https://hub.example.com,https://studio.example.com
# LANTERN_RUM_QUOTA_PER_MINUTE=120

# --- the dev-only Docker-socket collector ------------------------------------------------------
#
# OFF, and boot REFUSES to turn it on outside NODE_ENV=development: a container holding
# /var/run/docker.sock holds root on the host and can bind-mount `/` into a privileged container.
# The primary collection path is OTLP push from the collector to POST /otlp/v1/logs; this is a
# dev fallback only.
# LANTERN_DOCKER_COLLECTOR=false
# LANTERN_DOCKER_SOCKET=/var/run/docker.sock

# --- linking a trace id to a viewer ------------------------------------------------------------
#
# Where a trace id becomes a link. Optional: a deploy without Tempo publishes no link rather than a
# broken one. `{traceId}` is substituted; without it the id is appended.
# LANTERN_TRACE_URL_TEMPLATE=https://tempo.example.com/trace/{traceId}

# --- not read by this service ------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# OTEL_EXPORTER_OTLP_ENDPOINT and the rest of OTEL_* are read by the OpenTelemetry SDK loaded ahead
# of the process, not by src/env.ts. They belong in the deploy, not here.
#
# LANTERN_TEST_DATABASE_URL is read only by the test suite, and only when its name contains "test" —
# the database-backed tests truncate. CI exports it; a different spelling would silently skip them.
